    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's dive straight into the chemistry. The Maillard reaction begins when the amino group (-NH2) of an amino acid acts as a nucleophile. This means it donates a pair of electrons to form a bond. In the case of the Maillard reaction, the amino group targets the carbonyl group (C=O) of a reducing sugar, such as glucose.\n\nWhen the amino group attacks the carbonyl carbon, it forms a new bond, creating an intermediate called a glycosylamine. This is a key step in the reaction, as it sets the stage for further transformations. The glycosylamine then undergoes a series of rearrangements and breakdowns, leading to the formation of various flavor and aroma compounds that contribute to the browning and flavor of cooked foods.\n\nAs for the influence of pH levels, they can affect the rate of the Maillard reaction by altering the ionization state of the amino acids and sugars involved. At lower pH levels, the amino groups are more likely to be protonated, making them less nucleophilic and slowing down the reaction. Conversely, at higher pH levels, the amino groups are more likely to be deprotonated, making them more",
